<spell-page "tree-diagram usage" notebook>
	<header level="1">
		Tree diagram
		<header>A tree diagram draws a tree:  a box for each node, a line to each of its children.</>
	</>
	<section>
		<toolbar class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#from-script">From script</>
			<item link="#clicks-and-folding">Clicks and folding</>
			<item link="#size">Size</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  the data contract, the two ways to give it, sizing, framework snippets, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			A tree diagram draws any tree:  it knows nothing about where the tree came from.
			Write it as nested tags:  a `<ui-tree-node>` inside the diagram, the nodes under it inside that.
			In Spell it's `<tree-node "If">`, its label a bare quoted name.

			| Attribute | What it is |
			|---|---|
			| `label` | The box's text, e.g. `If`, `Variable number`, `Number 15`.  None:  the node's own text. |
			| `detail` | A second, smaller, muted line under the label, e.g. a datatype `number`. |
			| `edge-label` | Text on the line from its parent, e.g. `condition`, `body`, `otherwise`. |
			| `hover-text` | What shows when the pointer rests on its box, e.g. the source it came from. |
			| `color` | The box's colour, a hue of the theme's:  `green`, `blue` ... |
			| `kind` | A word for what sort of node it is:  its box is also part `kind-<word>`, for your own style. |
			| `folded` | It starts folded:  the nodes under it don't show. |

			Anything else inside a node shows in its box:  an icon, a button, an image.
			It's a real element on the page, measured so the box fits it, and it keeps its state and events.

			Or give the tree as data, the same fields in a `TreeDiagramNode` (exported by `spell-ui`):
			`edgeLabel` for `edge-label`, `title` for `hover-text`, and `children`, left to right.

			- The `tree` property, from script or a framework.  It redraws whenever `tree` is set again:  set a NEW
			  tree, since a tree changed in place isn't seen.
			- A `<ui-json>` child, holding it as JSON in its text or in a `source` file.
			- A `<script type="application/json">` child, the older way, for a static page with no script of its own
			  (it works from `file://` too).

			The property wins;  then nested tags;  then JSON.  The diagram follows its children as the page changes them.
			Setting the property back to `undefined` shows the children's tree again.

			With no tree, or JSON that doesn't parse, it draws nothing and takes no room;  bad JSON also logs one console
			warning.  Data is checked, not trusted:  a node without a `label` gets an empty box, and anything that
			isn't a node is left out.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<ui-tree-diagram>
			  <ui-tree-node label="Add">
			    <ui-tree-node label="Number 1"></ui-tree-node>
			    <ui-tree-node label="Number 2"></ui-tree-node>
			  </ui-tree-node>
			</ui-tree-diagram>
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="From script">
		<markdown>
			Set `tree` on the element. In Solid, pass it as a plain prop, `tree={...}`:  the element takes the
			object whole, as its property.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			import "spell/ui/ui-tree-diagram"

			const diagram = document.querySelector("ui-tree-diagram")
			diagram.tree = {
			  label: "If",
			  children: [
			    { label: "IsA · integer", edgeLabel: "condition", detail: "boolean" },
			    { label: "Print", edgeLabel: "body" }
			  ]
			}
			```
		</>
		<markdown>
			```tsx
			import type { TreeDiagramNode } from "spell/ui"

			function ParseTree(props: { tree: TreeDiagramNode }) {
			  return <ui-tree-diagram tree={props.tree} />
			}
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Clicks and folding">
		<markdown>
			A click on a node sends `ui-node-clicked`, with its `node` and its `path`, its child index at each depth (`0.2.1`).
			`clickable` makes that clear to the reader:  a pointer cursor, a hover look, and keys.

			`foldable` gives each node with children a button;  `ui-node-toggled` says which, and `folded`.
			A folded node stays folded when the tree changes around it.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Size">
		<markdown>
			A tree is laid out tidy:  each depth a row (a column, `left-right`), each parent centred over its children,
			small subtrees spread evenly between big ones, and no two boxes or edge labels overlapping.

			Boxes fit their text, measured in its real font, so any font works.
			It's the page's by default;  `--ui-tree-diagram-font-family` sets another.
			A static server render has nothing to measure with, and estimates from the number of characters.

			It draws at its natural size, `--ui-tree-diagram-font-size` (13px in 16px text) for labels, and never
			larger.  In a narrower column it shrinks to fit, down to `--ui-tree-diagram-min-scale` (three quarters);
			a tree wider than that scrolls sideways inside the element instead of shrinking past readable.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			A plain tree diagram is a picture, and nothing in it takes focus.
			A tree wide enough to scroll scrolls with the scrollbar or a trackpad;  browsers that make scrolling boxes
			focusable also let the arrow keys scroll it.

			- `foldable`:  Tab reaches each fold button;  Enter or Space folds or opens it.
			- `clickable`:  Tab reaches each node too;  Enter or Space sends `ui-node-clicked`, as a click does.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- The drawing is one `<svg role="img">`, named by a summary of its root:  "Tree:  If, with 3 children"
			  (translatable).
			- `foldable` or `clickable` make it `role="group"`, as it holds buttons then:  each named by its node.
			- A node's `hover-text` is its box's `<title>`:  hover text for a pointer.
			- The summary doesn't read out the whole tree.  Where its shape matters to a reader, say it in the
			  text around the diagram too.
		</>
	</>
</spell-page>
